# Git Workflow

## Authority

- Read-only Git inspection is allowed when relevant.
- Do not commit, push, merge, rebase, amend, create or delete branches, or modify remotes unless the user explicitly requests it.
- Never modify local or global Git configuration.

## Before Mutation

- Inspect `git status --short --branch`.
- Review both staged and unstaged diffs.
- Inspect recent commit messages before choosing a commit style.
- Before pushing, confirm the current branch, upstream, remote, and ahead/behind state.

## Protect Work

- Preserve all user-authored and pre-existing changes.
- Stage only files belonging to the requested logical change.
- Avoid blanket staging when unrelated changes exist.
- Never commit secrets, environment files, credentials, private keys, generated runtime data, or ignored artifacts.
- Do not discard changes with `reset --hard`, `clean`, `checkout --`, or destructive `restore` unless explicitly approved.

## Commits

- Create commits only when explicitly requested.
- Keep each commit focused on one logical purpose.
- Follow repository history; when no style exists, use `type(scope): concise summary`.
- Focus the message on intent and reason, not a file list.
- Do not use `--no-verify`, bypass hooks, or disable signing requirements.
- Do not amend unless requested, or a newly created unpushed commit needs hook-generated files included.

## Push and History Rewriting

- Push only when explicitly requested.
- Never force-push `main` or `master`.
- Any other force push requires explicit approval and a warning about rewritten history.
- Avoid interactive Git commands that require unsupported terminal input.

## Verification

- After a commit or push, inspect status and the resulting commit.
- Report the commit hash, branch, push state, and remaining changes.
- Never claim a commit or push succeeded without verifying it.
